Docker Day 13 – Environment Variables & Multi-Service Docker Compose

Overview

Today I practiced managing application configuration using environment variables and Docker Compose. I also learned how multiple containers communicate with each other through a shared Docker network.

The main goal was to understand how application configuration reaches a container and how different services work together.

1. Environment Variables

Environment variables are configuration values that an application can read while running.

I practiced setting variables in the terminal:

export DB_HOST=localhost
echo $DB_HOST

- "export" makes a variable available to processes started from the current shell.
- "echo $DB_HOST" displays its value.

Environment variables are useful because configuration can be changed without modifying application code.

For example, database connection details can change between development and production environments.

2. Creating a ".env" File

I created a ".env" file to keep multiple configuration values together.

Example:

DB_HOST=localhost
DB_USER=aman
DB_NAME=notesdb

Instead of writing these values directly into application configuration, we can keep them separate.

In real applications, this approach is useful for managing database connection settings. Actual passwords and secrets must not be committed to GitHub.

3. Understanding "compose.yaml"

I created a "compose.yaml" file to define a Docker Compose service.

Docker Compose uses this file to understand which services to run, which images to use, and how to configure containers.

I used variable substitution:

environment:
  DB_HOST: ${DB_HOST}
  DB_USER: ${DB_USER}
  DB_NAME: ${DB_NAME}

Here, "${DB_HOST}" tells Compose to read the corresponding configuration value and pass it into the container environment.

The ".env" file provides values, while "compose.yaml" defines how those values are used.

4. Validating Docker Compose Configuration

I practiced:

sudo docker compose config

This command checks the Compose configuration and displays the resolved configuration.

It helped me verify that values from ".env" were correctly substituted.

I also learned that Compose automatically looks for default filenames such as "compose.yaml". For a differently named file, we can specify it using the "-f" option.

Example:

sudo docker compose -f myfile.yaml config

5. Testing Environment Variables Inside a Container

I used the Alpine Linux image as a lightweight test environment.

My Compose service included:

test:
  image: alpine
  environment:
    DB_HOST: ${DB_HOST}
    DB_USER: ${DB_USER}
    DB_NAME: ${DB_NAME}
  command: sh -c "env | grep '^DB_'"

I ran:

sudo docker compose run --rm test

The container displayed the database configuration values.

This confirmed that the variables were not only available in the Compose configuration but were also passed into the running container.

The "--rm" option automatically removes the temporary container after the command finishes.

6. Environment Variable Override

I tested how a shell environment variable can override a value from ".env".

sudo DB_USER=developer docker compose config

My ".env" file contained "DB_USER=aman", but the resolved configuration displayed "developer".

This helped me understand configuration precedence: an environment variable supplied in the shell takes priority over the value in the ".env" file for Compose interpolation.

The original ".env" file was not modified.

7. Working with Multiple Services

I updated my Compose file to define three services:

- "test" – Used to verify environment variables.
- "web" – Used the "nginx:alpine" image to run a web server.
- "client" – Used Alpine to send an HTTP request to the web service.

This helped me understand that one Compose project can manage multiple independent containers.

Each service has its own responsibility, but Compose manages them together.

8. Container-to-Container Communication

I configured the client service to request:

command: wget -qO- http://web

I also used:

depends_on:
  - web

The "depends_on" setting controls startup order by starting the web service before the client service.

I ran:

sudo docker compose run --rm client

The client successfully received the Nginx HTML response.

This demonstrated that services in the same default Compose network can communicate using their service names.

I also learned that "localhost" inside a container refers to that same container, not another container.

For example, a Flask application connecting to a PostgreSQL service should normally use "db" as its database hostname if the Compose service is named "db".

9. Understanding Database and Docker Volume

I learned that a database and a Docker volume have different responsibilities.

- A database organizes and manages application data.
- A Docker volume provides persistent storage for data files.

If a PostgreSQL container is deleted but its volume is retained, a new PostgreSQL container can reuse that volume and access the existing database data, provided it is configured compatibly.

This is important because database data should not depend only on a container's temporary filesystem.

10. Flask and PostgreSQL Architecture

I learned how Flask and PostgreSQL work together in a real application.

- Flask handles application requests and backend logic.
- PostgreSQL manages and stores application data.
- A Docker network allows the containers to communicate.
- A Docker volume helps preserve database data.
- Environment variables provide database connection configuration.

For example, when a user saves a note on a website, the Flask backend receives the request and sends the appropriate database operation to PostgreSQL.

The user does not need to access the database or Docker terminal directly.

11. Container Status and Cleanup

I practiced checking service status:

sudo docker compose ps

This helped me identify which containers were running.

Finally, I cleaned up the Compose project:

sudo docker compose down

This stopped and removed the Compose containers and its project network. It did not delete my ".env" or "compose.yaml" files.

Key Learning

Today I understood how Docker Compose handles configuration, how environment variables reach containers, and how multiple services communicate through a shared network.

I also learned why application services and database services have separate responsibilities and why persistent storage is important.

